EXT. MAYA'S BACKYARD - SUNRISE

The first golden light of morning filters through the trees, illuminating a backyard transformed into a custom training course. Plywood ramps, curved wooden banks, and steep channels twist across the lawn, mimicking a miniature concrete riverbed.

MAYA (9), a South Asian girl with curly hair tied high, sits in her lightweight manual wheelchair at the top of the highest ramp. She wears a vibrant teal windbreaker and fingerless gloves. Her wheelchair features bright orange spoke guards and a custom toolkit strapped under the seat.

Maya holds her totem, a small adjustable wrench on a lanyard around her neck. She kisses it for good luck, then tucks it inside her windbreaker.

MAYA
(whispering)
Three, two, one... go.

She pushes off. The wheelchair flies down the ramp. Maya leans into the curve, carving a perfect turn on a wooden bank.

She reaches a flat patch of grass. With practiced grace, Maya locks her brakes, slides forward, and transfers out of her wheelchair onto the soft grass. She stretches her legs forward, massaging her calf muscles with determination.

MAYA
(to herself)
Stronger every day. Just like we practiced.

She looks at the wooden ramps, visualizing the flow of water. She transfers back into her wheelchair with a smooth, effortless swing of her arms.

INT. GARAGE - DAY

The garage is Maya's engineering sanctuary. Tools hang in neat rows on pegboards. A shelf holds various wheels, gears, and castor forks.

Maya rolls up to her heavy wooden workbench. She locks her wheels, slides forward, and transfers onto a high wooden stool, resting her feet on the stool's lower rung.

She spreads a large map of the town's concrete drainage channels across the workbench. Her finger traces the steep slopes of Main Street leading down to the central drainage canal.

MAYA
(muttering)
If the storm hits the ridge, the runoff will turn Main Street into a rapid. The high curb at Second Avenue will act as a dam, diverting the water straight into the park.

She grabs her pencil and sketches three moves ahead on the map, outlining a safe path through the town's concrete grid.

She picks up her adjustable wrench and begins tightening a new custom steel bumper on the front of her wheelchair frame.

MAYA
(smiling)
There. Ready for anything.

INT. SCHOOL HALLWAY - DAY

The school hallway is bustling with kids locker-hunting before the bell.

Maya rolls down the hallway with ease, navigating the crowd of sneakers and backpacks. As an active wheelchair user with a spinal cord injury, she has engineered her chair to be a perfect extension of her personal space.

Her friends LEO (9) and CHLOE (9) walk beside her.

LEO
Did you see the weather forecast? They're saying it's the biggest storm of the summer.

CHLOE
I hope they cancel math class. We could go down to the park and watch the concrete canals fill up!

Maya stops her wheelchair, looking at Chloe with a serious expression.

MAYA
No way. The drainage canal is incredibly dangerous during a storm. The water rises faster than you can run. Promise me you'll stay away from the park.

LEO
Come on, Maya, it's just a little rain.

MAYA
(planning ahead)
It's not just rain. The town's concrete channels are designed to clear the streets, but they concentrate the water. It's a trap. Promise me.

Chloe and Leo exchange a look, then nod.

CHLOE
Okay, okay. We promise.

INT. MAYA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - AFTERNOON

Heavy rain lashes against the living room windows. The sky outside is a dark, ominous gray.

On the television, a local news anchor speaks with high energy.

NEWS ANCHOR
(from TV)
The flash flood warning is now active for the entire valley. Residents are urged to stay indoors and avoid all low-lying areas.

HELEN, Maya's mom, is packing a flashlight, a warm sweater, and some bottled water into an emergency backpack. She looks out the window, worried.

HELEN
Maya! We need to make sure the flashlights are working. And did we secure the backyard?

Maya rolls into the living room from the garage, her teal windbreaker already zipped up to her chin. She looks completely calm.

MAYA
Already done, Mom. I put fresh batteries in the flashlights, cleared the gutters, and secured the garage door. We have sandbags at the back door too.

HELEN
(sighing with relief)
What would I do without my chief engineer? You're always three moves ahead of everyone else.

MAYA
Just being prepared. I'm going to check on the street's drainage grate. If it gets blocked, the water will back up into Mr. Davis's yard.

HELEN
Be careful, Maya. Don't stay out long.

EXT. MAIN STREET - AFTERNOON

The rain is falling steadily now. Streams of water rush along the curbs, carrying leaves and trash toward the drains.

Maya rolls down the sidewalk, her orange spoke guards flashing through the gray rain. She navigates the wet concrete with absolute precision, keeping her momentum steady.

As she approaches the high curb at Second Avenue, MR. DAVIS, an elderly neighbor wearing a bright yellow raincoat, runs out of his house, splashing through the puddles.

MR. DAVIS
Oh, let me help you with that, Maya! It's dangerous out here in the wet!

Mr. Davis reaches out to grab the handles of Maya's wheelchair.

MAYA
(firmly)
Please don't touch my chair, Mr. Davis. I've got this.

Mr. Davis hesitates, stepping back.

MR. DAVIS
Are you sure? It's a high curb.

Maya leans forward, grips her handrims, and with a swift, powerful pop, lifts her front casters. She glides over the high curb effortlessly, landing smoothly on the asphalt.

MR. DAVIS
Wow. That was incredible.

MAYA
(smiling)
Just physics. Get inside, Mr. Davis! The water is rising fast!

EXT. TOWN PARK - AFTERNOON

The storm has intensified into a torrential downpour. The park's paved pathways have turned into rushing streams of brown water, creating natural ramps and channels.

Maya uses her backyard training. She maneuvers through the rushing water, carving turns and maintaining her balance against the strong current. She is in her element, navigating the obstacles with absolute control.

Suddenly, a sharp, panicked scream echoes from the direction of the main drainage canal.

MAYA
(gasping)
Leo? Chloe?

She spins her wheels and speeds toward the sound.

EXT. DRAINAGE CANAL - AFTERNOON

The canal is a massive concrete trench designed to carry runoff out of the town. Now, it is a raging river of brown, swirling water.

Leo and Chloe are trapped on a narrow concrete maintenance ledge halfway down the canal wall. The water is rising rapidly, splashing at their sneakers.

Just downstream, a massive pile of trash, branches, and wooden boards is jammed tight against the heavy iron grate of the main drain.

LEO
(screaming)
Help! The water is too fast!

CHLOE
(crying)
We can't climb up! The ramp is too slippery!

Maya rolls up to the edge of the canal. She looks at the scene, instantly planning three moves ahead.

MAYA
(shouting)
Hold on! I'm coming!

EXT. DRAINAGE CANAL - CONTINUOUS

Maya steers her wheelchair down the concrete access ramp. The water rushes over her wheels, but her engineered tires grip the concrete.

She fights the current, pushing hard with her gloved hands. She reaches the concrete ledge where Leo and Chloe are shivering.

MAYA
Grab onto the back of my chair! One at a time!

LEO
What about the grate? If it stays blocked, the whole ledge will go under!

Maya looks at the massive logjam blocking the grate. The manual release lever is on the far side of the metal grate, surrounded by deep, swirling water.

MAYA
I'll clear the grate. Leo, help Chloe climb up the ramp behind me. Go!

Maya turns her chair and pushes toward the iron grate. The current is incredibly strong.

EXT. DRAINAGE CANAL - GRATE - CONTINUOUS

Maya reaches the grate. She reaches out to pull a branch from the logjam, but a sudden surge of water pushes a heavy wooden pallet directly into her wheelchair.

With a sharp metallic crunch, the front left caster of Maya's wheelchair gets wedged tight between the iron bars of the grate and the heavy wooden pallet.

Maya pulls back on her handrims. The wheels spin uselessly in the rushing water. She is stuck.

The water level rises rapidly, now reaching Maya's chest. Leo and Chloe are still struggling to climb the wet ramp as the water climbs higher.

CHLOE
Maya! The water is too high!

Maya looks at her trapped wheelchair, then at the manual release lever just ten feet away across the slippery, flooded grate.

Instead of panicking, Maya remains completely calm. She looks down at the wedged caster, then at her windbreaker.

She reaches under her windbreaker and pulls out her totem—the small adjustable wrench on its lanyard.

MAYA
(to herself)
Three moves ahead.

With steady hands, she fits the wrench onto the quick-release bolt of her wedged front caster. She turns the wrench with a powerful twist.

The bolt yields. With a final turn, the caster slips free from the frame, remaining trapped in the grate.

Maya is free. She leans back, lifting her front frame. She balances perfectly on her two large rear wheels, executing a flawless wheelie in the rushing current.

She rolls forward on her two rear wheels, navigating the wet grate with absolute control.

Maya reaches the manual release lever. Still balancing on two wheels, she grabs the heavy iron handle with both hands and pulls down with all her might.

The locking mechanism disengages with a loud CLANG.

The heavy iron grate swings open. The massive pile of trash and debris is instantly sucked down into the main drain.

The floodwaters rapidly recede, rushing down the drain.

EXT. DRAINAGE CANAL - AFTERNOON

The rain has stopped. Sunbeams break through the thinning gray clouds, casting a warm glow over the wet concrete.

Maya sits in her wheelchair on the concrete ledge, balancing comfortably with her one remaining front caster, her sneakers safe and dry.

Leo and Chloe run down the ramp and throw their arms around her.

LEO
You did it! You saved us!

CHLOE
That was amazing! How did you free the chair?

Maya holds up her small adjustable wrench, a big grin on her face.

MAYA
Just a little engineering.

Leo and Chloe look at her with deep admiration.

MAYA
Come on. Let's head to the garage. I have a spare caster with my name on it.

Maya rolls up the ramp with Leo and Chloe, completely at ease in her engineered chair, ready to build her next great invention.